  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe need to formulate and solve a step function minimization problem using a two-stage optimization approach. The first stage uses a fixed pulse-count representation to handle the full state space efficiently, while the second stage refines the configuration via local search. The core idea is to:\n\n- Disentangle the pulse-count and pulse-position optimization\n- Use integer linear programming (ILP) to find the optimal pulse placement for fixed pulse-count configurations\n- Allow iterative refinement by adapting pulse-count based on overlap analysis\n- Leverage the initial parent solution as a warm start\n\nThis is substantially different from previous approaches by:\n- Using an endogenous representation strategy that auto-generates candidates\n- Formulating the problem as a constrained parameter optimization with integral/l1-norm\n- Employing gradient-based local search starting from discrete samples\n- Incorporating a fallback mechanism to maintain validity during resampling\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nRepresent the step function by:\n  - Number of pulses k (with constraint k <= 10)\n  - Widths w_i for each pulse (w_i <= n_points/4)\n  - Positions p_j for each pulse (p_j in [0.1, 0.9] scaled to n_points)\n\nObjective: minimize \n  C_5 = max_k integral h(x) * (1 - h(x+k)) dx\n\nsubject to:\n  \u2211h[i] = n/2, 0 <= h[i] <= 1\n  integral(h) = 1\n\nFormulate constraints with:\n  - Activation indicators for pulses (binary variables)\n  - Height/width feasibility\n  - Sum calculations over continuous space\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n  a. Initialize: Load parent or generate random candidate if parent unavailable.\n  b. Set up objects and donate constraints\n  c. For each resolution level (70, 140, 280,...):\n        Convert parent to current resolution\n        Use active set method to determine pulse-count\n        Run ILP relaxation to compute optimal positions\n        Post-process to enforce binary values\n        Perform local search to refine solution\n        Evaluate and record score\n  d. Maintain best candidate across resolutions\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   Default pulse-count selection: Start with parent pulse-count, then adjust based on score\n   Pulse-parameterization: Rectangular pulses with quadratic avoidance for overlap\n   Local search: SLSQP with treatment constraint for killing stagnant regions\n\n5. Budget and Robustness\n   For n=70: Target 10 candidates/level\n   For n=200: Target 5 candidates/level\n   Monitor resource usage and terminate scatters too long\n   Maintain fallback solution derived from parent\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   Ensure: \n     - All arrays meet sum and bounds\n     - Scores are finite and match evaluation\n   Returns (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) where:\n     - h_values = numpy array of [0,1] with exactly n/2 ones\n     - c5_bound = maximum correlation * dx\n     - n_points = number of samples used in evaluation",
